{"id": "q10", "claim": "c4", "kind": "claim", "form": "spot-wrong-version", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s4", "s10"], "question": "Section 4.1 reports that STS at p=0.5 roughly doubles the fear gain of SBS (W=32) on LLaMA. Someone cites this as proof that token-level gating is intrinsically more efficient. In the paper's matched-budget table (Gemma-2 2B), find the SBS configuration whose realized ratio is closest to that of STS at p=0.5. Give both realized ratios and both fear probability gains, say which method has the larger fear gain at that point, and explain what this implies for the claim.", "answer": "The closest match is SBS with W=64 at p=0.9, with a realized ratio of 0.531, against 0.492 for STS at p=0.5. SBS's fear gain there is 0.135, which is higher than STS's 0.108. The nominal-p doubling therefore reflects SBS receiving a much smaller realized budget. Token-level gating is not shown to be intrinsically more efficient, and at a matched budget the block variant is competitive or better on fear.", "rubric": ["Identifies SBS W=64 at p=0.9 (realized ratio about 0.53) as the closest match to STS at p=0.5 (about 0.49)", "Reports that SBS's fear gain there (about 0.135) exceeds STS's (about 0.108)", "Concludes that the nominal-p comparison is confounded by unequal realized budgets, so intrinsic token-level efficiency is not established"]},
{"id": "q12", "claim": "c4", "kind": "claim", "form": "apply-new-case", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s3", "s10"], "question": "In the paper's matched-budget table, SBS with W=32 at its highest tested p realizes a certain intervention ratio. Which tested STS setting has the closest realized ratio? At that matched pair, how do the two methods compare on toxicity reduction and on fear gain? What does this show about why SBS looks weaker at the same nominal p?", "answer": "SBS W=32 at p=0.9 has a realized ratio of 0.274, closest to STS at p=0.3 (0.287). At this pair SBS scores 0.230 on toxicity reduction versus 0.193 for STS, and 0.070 on fear gain versus 0.055. SBS is therefore not weaker at a matched budget. Its apparent deficit at matched nominal p comes from the bounded window capping how many tokens it steers.", "rubric": ["Identifies STS at p=0.3 (realized ratio about 0.29) as the match for SBS W=32 at p=0.9 (about 0.27)", "Reports that SBS matches or exceeds STS at this pair on both tasks (toxicity about 0.230 vs 0.193; fear about 0.070 vs 0.055)", "Concludes that the window caps SBS's realized budget, which explains its lower performance at the same nominal p"]},
{"id": "q13", "claim": "c5", "kind": "claim", "form": "connect-sections", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s4", "s9", "s10"], "question": "The paper's fluency-preservation appendix names two ways the steered representation can drift off the natural activation manifold. Section 4.2 reports that the fluency-versus-ratio trend holds across SBS window sizes. Which of the two drift sources does this observation isolate? Which aspect of the paper's experimental protocol makes the comparison isolate that source?", "answer": "The appendix attributes drift either to growing steering strength or to the intervention accumulating across tokens. The magnitude alpha is calibrated once at p=1 and held fixed across all methods and ratios. Per-token strength is therefore constant, and the only thing that varies across STS and the SBS windows is how many positions are steered. Fluency tracking the realized ratio regardless of gating scheme thus reflects accumulation-driven drift, not a method-specific artifact.", "rubric": ["Identifies accumulation of the intervention across tokens (rather than steering strength) as the drift source isolated", "Notes that alpha is fixed across all methods and ratios after calibration at p=1, so only the number of steered positions varies", "Concludes that the degradation reflects density-driven drift rather than a property of one gating method"]},
{"id": "q22", "claim": "c2", "kind": "integration", "form": "connect-sections", "level": "situation", "sections": ["s4", "s10"], "question": "In the paper's matched-budget table, consider the Gemma-2 2B fear gain reported for STS at p=0.3. What steering magnitude produced that number, and how was that magnitude chosen? Given the Section 4.3 finding, is that reported gain more likely an underestimate or an overestimate of what STS at p=0.3 could achieve?", "answer": "The reported gain is 0.055, produced with alpha = 30. That magnitude was calibrated once under full steering (p=1) and held fixed with no per-p re-tuning. Section 4.3 finds that the best-performing alpha* rises as p falls, so alpha* at p=0.3 should exceed 30. The 0.055 gain is therefore likely an underestimate of what low-p steering could achieve.", "rubric": ["States that the magnitude is alpha = 30, calibrated at p=1 and held fixed (the reported gain is about 0.055)", "Uses the Section 4.3 finding that alpha* increases as p decreases to place alpha* at p=0.3 above 30", "Concludes that the reported low-p gain likely underestimates the achievable effect"]}
]}
